data: {"type": "assistant_msg", "ts": "2026-08-06T12:27:26.970465+00:00", "content": "Based on the live telemetry gathered for **ce_branch24**, here is the prediction:\n\n### Next Likely Failure Window\n**Within the next 6\u201312 minutes (i.e., around the next polling cycle after ts \u2248 1786019128\u20131786019788).**\n\n### Why \u2014 the fault pattern in evidence\n\n| Metric | What the series shows | Key evidence |\n|--------|-----------------------|--------------|\n| **Jitter spikes (all 5 hubs)** | Every 2\u20133 sample intervals (\u2248 30\u201390 s) each tunnel sees a burst where `sdwan_tunnel_jitter_ms` jumps from ~2\u201310 ms to **50\u201380 ms** (hub1), **40\u201370 ms** (hub2), **60\u201380 ms** (hub3), **55\u201365 ms** (hub4), **55\u201360 ms** (hub5). | [metrics:0]\u2013[metrics:4] (hub1), [metrics:20]\u2013[metrics:24] (hub2), [metrics:40]\u2013[metrics:44] (hub3), [metrics:60]\u2013[metrics:64] (hub4), [metrics:80]\u2013[metrics:84] (hub5) |\n| **Packet-loss bursts** | Co-incident with the jitter spikes, `sdwan_tunnel_loss_pct` leaps from <0.2 % to **27\u201350 %** on multiple tunnels (hub2 at ts 1786018708\u20138738, hub3 at 1786018648\u20138678, hub5 now creeping up at 1786019068). | [metrics:28]\u2013[metrics:29] (hub2), [metrics:46]\u2013[metrics:47] (hub3), [metrics:99] (hub5 latest) |\n| **Latency stair-step rise** | On every tunnel the baseline `sdwan_tunnel_latency_ms` has been **climbing 2\u20135 ms per interval** for the last 6\u20138 samples (e.g., hub1 from 69 ms \u2192 79 ms, hub4 from 64 ms \u2192 111 ms). | [metrics:5]\u2013[metrics:19] (hub1), [metrics:65]\u2013[metrics:79] (hub4) |\n| **No log events** | `search_logs` returned zero error/flap/rekey lines \u2014 the path is not hard-down, it is **degrading softly** until a congestion-collapse or buffer-exhaustion threshold is crossed. | [events:no rows] |\n\n### Root-cause hypothesis\nThe **synchronised, periodic jitter/loss spikes across all five hub tunnels** point to a **single upstream congestion hotspot** (likely the branch\u2019s local access circuit or the first-hop provider edge) that fills its egress queue every 30\u201390 s. Each fill/drain cycle creates the observed burst; the queues are now draining more slowly (rising baseline latency), so the next fill cycle will almost certainly push loss > 5 % on multiple tunnels simultaneously \u2014 the typical SD-WAN path-failover threshold.\n\n### Blast-radius check\nTopology walk shows **pe12** (first core hop) is healthy (0 % loss, 1.6 % CPU) and **ce_branch12** (neighbor branch) has clean tunnels (0.14 % loss, 9 ms jitter) \u2014 so the fault is **local to ce_branch24\u2019s access**, not the core. [topo:ce_branch24], [topo:pe12], [topo:ce_branch12]\n\n### Recommended immediate action\n1. **Open a ticket with the access-circuit provider** citing \u201cperiodic 30\u201390 s congestion bursts, rising baseline latency, multi-hub impact.\u201d  \n2. **Pre-stage a failover** to the secondary access circuit (if dual-homed) or LTE backup before the next burst window.  \n3. **Enable continuous 10 s polling** on `sdwan_tunnel_loss_pct` / `sdwan_tunnel_jitter_ms` for ce_branch24 to catch the exact minute the next spike hits."}

